  "body": "## Exact result and source scope\n\nThis independently reviewed candidate answers the source's **existential growing-seed question**: for every unbounded finite-second-moment degree law supported on integers at least two, and every fixed positive rate disadvantage, a deterministic slowly diverging degree threshold can select seeds with vanishing vertex density and vanishing half-edge density while the slower infection captures 1-o_P(1) of vertices.\n\nSeeds are selected using degrees before pairing or passage-time sampling. The faster infection starts uniformly within a fixed low-degree class. The proof uses a protected finite path, a joint fixed-time local exploration, and a justified deterministic diagonal schedule.\n\n**Scope exclusions are essential:** no single-hub theorem, prescribed seed budget, sharp degree/rate threshold, or graph/weight-aware adversarial-root guarantee is claimed. The original OWR paragraph and supporting paper explicitly allow initial counts growing with n; the independent reviewer separately verified that source interpretation. Existing branching-exploration methods and random-regular growing-seed results are credited; historical priority is unestablished.\n\nRecommended status: **claimed_solved, 2/5 approaches**, for the stated existential regime.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe frozen proof is unchanged. All 11,561 author assertions reproduce byte-identically. Separate adversarial AI review passed, with 62,001 independent assertions over 9,999 finite weighted competitions; eight unchanged review files are included. Finite controls do not replace the asymptotic probability proof. No human peer-review claim.\n\nOnly this problem's research folder is changed. Queue status is coordinated separately.\n",
